'''Regional differences''' are differences between the [[Nyanko Daisensou|Japanese]], English, Taiwanese and Korean versions of [[The Battle Cats]], ranging from cosmetic changes to exclusive content.

This list may be incomplete; if you know of any more differences, add them to the articles.

==General==
*Each version has its own server and runs on a different schedule. While the schedules of the English, Taiwanese and Korean versions are practically the same, the Japanese version is completely different, most notably in how it receives major updates 3-5 weeks ahead of other versions and new [[Event Stages]] anywhere from a few days to months before they're localized. [[Anniversary]] events in particular can take years to appear in other versions, as the Japanese version is the oldest and the farthest ahead.
**Occasionally, the differing schedules can result in enemies having different debut stages between versions, such as [[Devil Wife]]'s first appearance being [[Attack of the Omens]] in BCJP but [[Cats Cradle Basin]] elsewhere.
*Names and descriptions can range from slightly to drastically different between languages, usually due to cultural differences, character limits or puns, wordplays or references that don't translate well.
*[[Ms. Sign]], [[Angel Fanboy]] and [[Aku Researcher (Rare Cat)|Aku Researcher]]'s signs as well as [[Kyuubei]]'s dialogue are always translated to the appropriate language. However, this is not the case for text on Evil Aku Researcher's sign, [[Aku Cyclone]]'s ring, the ''World of Darkness'' [[Meow Medals|Meow Medal]], etc., which is rendered in upside-down katakana regardless of version.

==Japanese (BCJP)==
*The main currency used during [[Battle|battle]] is yen (円).
*Instead of the world, [[Empire of Cats]] takes place across the 47 prefectures of Japan, with [[wikipedia:Iriomote Island|Iriomote Island]] as stage 48; in turn, all the enemy bases, [[Treasure]] names, Treasure set designs and Empire of Cats [[Meow Medals|Meow Medal]] icons are entirely different.
**Some of the [[backgrounds]] are also different, though there are none that can't be found in the other Empires of Cats.
**While most of the bases do not appear outside of the [[Main Menu]] in the other versions, [[Egypt (Empire of Cats)|Kyoto]]'s base was reused for the [[Japan (Empire of Cats)|Japan]] stage.
**The [[Korea (Empire of Cats)|first stage]] of Empire of Cats, Nagasaki Prefecture, always uses a castle tower with a red flag as its enemy base. In other versions, stage 1's enemy base changes to either [[wikipedia:Gwanghwamun|Gwanghwamun]] (BCEN/BCKR) or [[wikipedia:Taipei 101|Taipei 101]] (BCTW) after the first clear.
**[[The Aku Realms]] also takes place in Japan, with stage 30 being [[wikipedia:Mount Fuji|Mt. Fuji]], a real location, instead of [[Mount Aku (Aku Realm)|Mount Aku]].
**Since [[Into the Future]] is the only set of chapters taking place across the world, the ''The Future is Ours'' and ''...And Stay Dead'' Meow Medals lack the UFOs that are present in the other versions.
*Into the Future's map lacks the blue tint that it has in other versions.
*[[Maneki Cat (Special Cat)|Maneki Cat]] was first obtainable via the [[Cat Beacon]] feature, whereas in other versions it was only obtainable from the [[What's in the Box (Event Gacha)|What's in the Box]] Event Gacha.
*The exclusive stamp reward unit [[Nora (Special Cat)|Nora]] was created as part of a tie-in with the Battle Cats manga, [[Nyanko Daisensou ~Nippon Shinryaku Hiroku~]].
*To promote the Japan-exclusive Battle Cats gummies, this version had the stage [[Cola Flavor Cat|Nyanko Great Gummy War]] and the paid [[:Category:Special Cats|Special Cat]] [[Gummy Cat (Special Cat)|Gummy Cat]]. Likewise, the Battle Cats wafers were promoted with [[Wafer Cat Advent]] and the unit [[Wafer Cat (Special Cat)|Wafer Cat]].
*To celebrate the Japanese [[Battle Cats TV]] channel's 150,000 subscriber milestone, [[Cat Boy (Special Cat)|Cat Boy]] could be obtained from September 19th, 2020 to September 29th, 2020.
*Every year from 2016 to 2019, players could get a free [[Cat Capsule#Rare Capsule|Rare Cat Ticket]] from the [[Sponsorship BitSummit Memorial!]] event stage, which was added to commemorate the BitSummit indie game festival in [[wikipedia:ja:京都市勧業館|Miyako Messe]].
*The [[Joy & Prosperity|Lunar New Year]] stage does not exist.
*[[Catburger (Special Cat)|Catburger]] drops from the [[What's in the Box (Event Gacha)|What's in the Box]] Event Gacha instead of the [[Faster Than Fast Food|Minced Meet-Up]] stage.
*[[Catburger (Special Cat)|Catburger]]'s [[True Form]] is unlocked using 1 Red [[Catfruit]] and 20,000 XP, rather than needing the player to beat an [[:Category:Awakening Stages|Awakening Stage]].
**Even though the [[Value Meal Cat (Enemy)|enemy version]] of Value Meal Cat doesn't exist in the Japanese version, there is an empty space for it between [[Gary Gory]] and [[Metal Snache]] in the [[Enemy Guide]].
*[[Li'l Titan Cat (Special Cat)|Li'l Jamiera Cat]]'s sweater says "I Love うに" (''uni'', meaning "sea urchin roe") instead of "I Love Crab".
*[[Li'l Valkyrie (Uber Rare Cat)|Wind Dancer Valkyrie]] and [[Li'l Valkyrie Dark (Uber Rare Cat)|Dark Rider Valkyrie]] are clad in traditional Chinese-style outfits, the same as in BCTW.
*[[Miss Haka]]'s grave is engraved with the kanji "墓" (''haka'', meaning "grave" or "tomb") instead of RIP (Rest In Peace).
*[[Madam Peng]], [[Countess Koryessa]] and [[High Countess Koryessa]] wear red lipstick.
*Most of the [[Merc Storia Collaboration Event|Merc Storia]] collab enemies have dialogue that appears in speech bubbles above them during [[battle]].
*The Treasure icons have the kanji "宝" (''takara'', meaning "treasure") instead of a star.
*When hacked into the Catfruit Storage, the three unused [[Catfruit]] types show as a white circle with the kanji "仮" (meaning "temporary") written over it instead of a black box.
*The first [[Wildcat Slots]] icon says 7th instead of 7.
*Each anniversary replaces some of the Wildcat Slots' icons, rather than using all the same icons every time. Later anniversaries also change the design and background of the Wildcat Slots themselves.
*Some text and imagery from the [[Anniversary/9th Year|9th anniversary]] says Q instead of 9. Q stands for "Quiz", the theme of the 9th anniversary event, and is pronounced the same as 九 (''kyū''), the Japanese word for 9.
*The [[wikipedia:Golden Week (Japan)|Golden Week]] celebration is much bigger; players get to enjoy [[Treasure Festival]]s, the Metal Festival, [[:Category:Monthly Event All-Stars Stages|event all-stars stages]] and even the Wildcat Slots during the event period.
*BCJP had events for some [[Million Downloads Event|Million Downloads]] milestones that were skipped in other versions, such as [[Million Downloads Event/33 Million|33M]].
